.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help (see a summary with \*(Aq\-h\*(Aq)
.SH "H5I BROWSER MESSAGE"
One stored message, as it went out or as it came back
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.SS SYNOPSIS
\fBmessage\fR [\fB\-\-part\fR] [\fB\-\-raw\fR] [\fB\-\-body\-to\fR] [\fB\-s\fR|\fB\-\-session\fR] [\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] <\fISEQ\fR> 
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.SS OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-part\fR \fI<HALF>\fR
Which half. Both by default
.br

.br
\fIPossible values:\fR
.RS 14
.IP \(bu 2
request
.IP \(bu 2
response
.IP \(bu 2
both
.RE
.TP
\fB\-\-raw\fR
Print it as an HTTP message rather than a summary.

Wins over `\-\-json`: a wire message is bytes, and bytes wrapped in a JSON string are no longer the message.
.TP
\fB\-\-body\-to\fR \fI<PATH>\fR
Write the body to this file, exactly as it came back.

The store already holds the bytes; this is the way to get them out. A response is not always something to read \(em a database backup left in an open bucket, an image, an archive \(em and the next step is usually a tool that wants a file.

With `\-\-part both`, the response body if there is one, otherwise the request\*(Aqs.
.TP
\fB\-s\fR, \fB\-\-session\fR \fI<NAME>\fR
Which session, when more than one is open
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR

.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help (see a summary with \*(Aq\-h\*(Aq)
.TP
<\fISEQ\fR>
The sequence number, as `requests` lists them
.SH "H5I BROWSER DIFF"
