.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.SS SYNOPSIS
\fBdiff\fR [\fB\-s\fR|\fB\-\-session\fR] [\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] <\fISEQ\fR> <\fISEQ\fR> 
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.SS OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-s\fR, \fB\-\-session\fR \fI<NAME>\fR
Which session, when more than one is open
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR

.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help (see a summary with \*(Aq\-h\*(Aq)
.TP
<\fISEQ\fR>
The response to compare from
.TP
<\fISEQ\fR>
The response to compare to
.SH "H5I BROWSER MATCH"
Ask a stored response a question, for a script to branch on
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.SS SYNOPSIS
\fBmatch\fR [\fB\-\-regex\fR] [\fB\-\-contains\fR] [\fB\-\-json\-path\fR] [\fB\-\-header\fR] [\fB\-\-status\fR] [\fB\-\-longer\-than\fR] [\fB\-\-shorter\-than\fR] [\fB\-s\fR|\fB\-\-session\fR] [\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] <\fISEQ\fR> 
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.SS OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-regex\fR \fI<PATTERN>\fR
A regular expression over the body. Capture groups come back
.TP
\fB\-\-contains\fR \fI<TEXT>\fR
A literal substring of the body. Needs no escaping, which matters when the thing being looked for is a payload
.TP
\fB\-\-json\-path\fR \fI<PATH[=VALUE]>\fR
A dotted path into a JSON body (`session.token`), or `path=value`
.TP
\fB\-\-header\fR \fI<NAME[=VALUE]>\fR
A header by name, or `name=value`
.TP
\fB\-\-status\fR \fI<CODE>\fR
The status code
.TP
\fB\-\-longer\-than\fR \fI<BYTES>\fR
The body is longer than this many bytes
.TP
\fB\-\-shorter\-than\fR \fI<BYTES>\fR
The body is shorter than this many bytes
.TP
\fB\-s\fR, \fB\-\-session\fR \fI<NAME>\fR
Which session, when more than one is open
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR

.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help (see a summary with \*(Aq\-h\*(Aq)
.TP
<\fISEQ\fR>
The response to ask about
.SH "H5I BROWSER RESEND"
Send one of this session's own requests again, with changes
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.SS SYNOPSIS
\fBresend\fR [\fB\-\-set\fR] [\fB\-\-set\-file\fR] [\fB\-\-unset\fR] [\fB\-\-create\fR] [\fB\-\-repeat\fR] [\fB\-\-race\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-follow\fR] [\fB\-\-reset\-budget\fR] [\fB\-\-as\fR] [\fB\-\-keep\-credentials\fR] [\fB\-\-raw\-target\fR] [\fB\-\-raw\-request\fR] [\fB\-s\fR|\fB\-\-session\fR] [\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] <\fISEQ\fR> 
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.SS OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-set\fR \fI<TARGET=VALUE>\fR
Change one part of it: `query.id=456`, `header.X\-Real\-IP=127.0.0.1`, `cookie.session=forged`, `json.role=admin`, `form.user=admin`, `path=/admin`, `method=POST`, `body.raw=<bytes>`. Repeatable, and applied in the order given.

The value is everything after the first `=`, so a payload full of `=` needs no escaping.
.TP
\fB\-\-set\-file\fR \fI<TARGET=PATH>\fR
Set one part of it from a file: `multipart.userfile=./payload.jpg`.

The value is the file\*(Aqs bytes, unaltered. `\-\-set` cannot carry them: a command line is text and a JPEG begins `ff d8`, which is not text in any encoding. A magic\-number check is a filter worth testing, so there has to be a way to send a real one.

Applied after every `\-\-set`, in the order given.
.TP
\fB\-\-unset\fR \fI<TARGET>\fR
Remove one part of it, by the same names
.TP
\fB\-\-create\fR
Add a target that is not there rather than refusing.

Off by default: a parameter that does not exist is usually a typo, and a typo that silently succeeds costs a whole turn spent reading a response that was never going to differ.
.TP
\fB\-\-repeat\fR \fI<N>\fR [default: 1]
Send it this many times and report the clock, for a timing test.

Repeated inside the engine rather than by a shell loop: the thing being measured is milliseconds and starting a process costs tens of them, so a loop out here would measure the loop. The reply carries every sample plus a median and a median absolute deviation, which is the pair that survives one scheduling hiccup where a mean does not.
.TP
\fB\-\-race\fR
Release the repeated sends together, for a race.

The test for check\-then\-act: an application that reads a balance, decides, and writes it back has a window between the read and the write, and `\-\-repeat 20 \-\-race` is what finds out how wide it is.

A burst, and named as one: the sends leave from twenty threads that meet at a barrier first. It is not a single\-packet attack, which needs each request split across two writes with the last byte held back. Ordinary check\-then\-act windows do not need that.

Every send is a receipt and a stored message like any other, so a race that reproduces is a race somebody else can read afterwards.
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-follow\fR
Stop at the first redirect and report it, rather than following it.

A browser follows a `Location`; a test usually wants the 302 itself. It is where an authentication flow says who you are, where an open redirect proves it accepts anything, and where the `Set\-Cookie` that logs you in actually rides. The reply carries the status and the headers with nothing followed.
.TP
\fB\-\-reset\-budget\fR
Start this page\*(Aqs network allowance again before sending.

The budget exists to bound *page* code: a script in a loop is the untrusted thing it stops. A blind extraction is the opposite, an agent deliberately sending hundreds of requests it composed, and without this it stops partway through with "this page has spent its allowance" and every later answer reads as a negative result.

Grants nothing new: navigating resets the same allowance, so this is the same act without throwing away the page.
.TP
\fB\-\-as\fR \fI<SESSION>\fR
Send it from another session instead of this one.

The authorization test, in one flag: take the request one logged\-in session made and send it from a different logged\-in session. The message comes from `\-\-session`; the cookies, identity, policy and receipts are the named session\*(Aqs.

The source session\*(Aqs own credentials are dropped, because carrying them would send a request that is neither user\*(Aqs and whose answer means nothing. `\-\-keep\-credentials` sends them anyway, for the test where that is the question.
.TP
\fB\-\-keep\-credentials\fR
Carry the source session\*(Aqs `Cookie` and `Authorization` headers into the other session. Only meaningful with `\-\-as`
.TP
\fB\-\-raw\-target\fR \fI<TARGET>\fR
Send this request\-target unchanged, without URL normalization.

Policy, budget, and receipt checks still apply. The sender computes `Host` and `Content\-Length`; use `\-\-raw\-request` to control framing.
.TP
\fB\-\-raw\-request\fR \fI<PATH>\fR
Send a complete request unchanged from this file; `\-` reads stdin.

The stored URL supplies the authority for policy checks and dialing.
.TP
\fB\-s\fR, \fB\-\-session\fR \fI<NAME>\fR
Which session, when more than one is open
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR

.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help (see a summary with \*(Aq\-h\*(Aq)
.TP
<\fISEQ\fR>
The sequence number to send again, as `requests` lists them
