Print help
.SH "H5I BROWSER VIEW"
Watch this session's page, and take the controls
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.SS SYNOPSIS
\fBview\fR [\fB\-s\fR|\fB\-\-session\fR] [\fB\-\-web\fR] [\fB\-\-port\fR] [\fB\-\-fps\fR] [\fB\-\-assume\-graphics\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] 
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.SS OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-s\fR, \fB\-\-session\fR \fI<NAME>\fR
Which session, when more than one is open. A name from `\-\-session` at open time, or an opaque id. Defaults to $H5I_BROWSER_SESSION, then to the session `open` last made
.TP
\fB\-\-web\fR
Serve the page to a browser over loopback instead of drawing it here.

For a terminal that cannot draw images, and for a page the keyboard cannot reach.
.TP
\fB\-\-port\fR \fI<PORT>\fR [default: 0]
Port for `\-\-web`. 0 picks a free one
.TP
\fB\-\-fps\fR \fI<N>\fR [default: 10]
Frame\-rate ceiling asked of the session
.TP
\fB\-\-assume\-graphics\fR
Skip the graphics probe and render anyway, for a terminal the probe gets wrong
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help (see a summary with \*(Aq\-h\*(Aq)
.SH "H5I BROWSER URL"
Print the loopback viewer URL for a box, token included. `h5i box view` is what actually serves it; this is for pasting into a browser when a forward is already running
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.SS SYNOPSIS
\fBurl\fR [\fB\-\-port\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] <\fINAME\fR> 
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.SS OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-port\fR \fI<PORT>\fR [default: 7331]

.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help
.TP
<\fINAME\fR>
A box name
.SH "H5I JOIN"
Open a box someone else is sharing, from a ticket they sent you
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.SS SYNOPSIS
\fBjoin\fR [\fB\-\-port\fR] [\fB\-\-shared\-jar\fR] [\fB\-\-bind\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] <\fITICKET\fR> 
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.SS OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-port\fR \fI<PORT>\fR [default: 0]
Local port to serve it on. 0 picks a free one and prints it
.TP
\fB\-\-shared\-jar\fR
Join even when the only address left is `127.0.0.1`
.TP
\fB\-\-bind\fR \fI<ADDR>\fR
Serve on this loopback address instead of a random `127.x.y.z`.

The address is bound exactly, no fallback, and only loopback (`127.0.0.0/8`) is accepted. This is the WSL answer: Windows forwards only `127.0.0.1` into the VM, so the private address a join normally picks binds fine and is then unreachable from a Windows browser. `\-\-bind 127.0.0.1` counts as shared\-jar consent by itself; any other loopback address keeps a cookie jar of its own.
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help (see a summary with \*(Aq\-h\*(Aq)
.TP
<\fITICKET\fR>
The `h5i1_...` ticket you were sent, or `\-` to read it from stdin.

`/proc/<pid>/cmdline` is world\-readable on an ordinary Linux box and this process runs for the whole session, so a ticket passed as an argument is legible to every other user on the machine for as long as you are joined, and a ticket is the whole authorization. `pbpaste | h5i join \-` keeps it out of the process table and your shell history.
.SH "H5I RUNNER"
Run boxes on another Linux machine you own
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.SS SYNOPSIS
\fBrunner\fR [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] <\fIsubcommands\fR>
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.SS OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help
.SH "H5I RUNNER PAIR"
Pair with a Linux machine so boxes can run on it
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.SS SYNOPSIS
\fBpair\fR [\fB\-\-port\fR] [\fB\-\-fingerprint\fR] [\fB\-\-print\-only\fR] [\fB\-\-worker\-path\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] <\fINAME\fR> <\fI[USER@]HOST\fR> 
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.SS OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-port\fR \fI<PORT>\fR
SSH port, when it is not 22
.TP
\fB\-\-fingerprint\fR \fI<SHA256:...>\fR
Refuse to pair unless the host key matches this fingerprint.

Pairing otherwise trusts the key the machine presents the first time, exactly like your first `ssh` to a new host. Read the real one on the machine itself with `ssh\-keygen \-lf /etc/ssh/ssh_host_ed25519_key.pub` and pass it here to close that window.
.TP
\fB\-\-print\-only\fR
Print the `authorized_keys` line instead of installing it.

For a runner where you add keys by another route, or where you want to read the line before it goes in.
.TP
\fB\-\-worker\-path\fR \fI<PATH>\fR
Path to `h5i` on the runner, when it is not on the PATH of the account you are pairing with
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help (see a summary with \*(Aq\-h\*(Aq)
.TP
<\fINAME\fR>
A short name for this machine, used in commands and output. It is a label: the runner\*(Aqs identity is its host key, so renaming or re\-pointing a name never moves a box to different hardware
.TP
<\fI[USER@]HOST\fR>
Where it is: `user@host`, or just `host` to use your SSH default
.SH "H5I RUNNER PROBE"
Ask a runner what it can do right now
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.SS SYNOPSIS
\fBprobe\fR [\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] <\fINAME\fR> 
.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.SS OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR

.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help (see a summary with \*(Aq\-h\*(Aq)
.TP
<\fINAME\fR>

